  "quote_spans": [
    {
      "chunk_id": 22,
      "char_start": 914,
      "char_end": 1447,
      "text": "His bearing, it is true, was rather arrogant, and his\ntongue not the most good-natured; but he was generally liked\nnevertheless, for he was kind-hearted, if he was only taken on the right\nside, and it did not seem to be his sailor-like qualities upon which he\nprided himself so much as upon the superior acuteness of his\nunderstanding, which he delighted to display in discussions with the\nred-bearded and somewhat consequential sailmaker, who had the reputation\nof being a well-read man, and who affected a proportionate importance"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 23,
      "char_start": 275,
      "char_end": 586,
      "text": "Up at Memel they had had great difficulties to contend with, owing to\nthe condition of the ice; and their bad luck seemed to be going to\nfollow them, for in the Skager Rack they found themselves suddenly\nwedged into a field of drift-ice, with the prospect of having to remain\nwhere they were for weeks perhaps"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 71,
      "char_start": 1146,
      "char_end": 1517,
      "text": "He had found a rancid salt-herring,\nset up on four legs with a tail, as he was walking on the poop one\nevening in the moonlight; and as complaints had been recently made about\nthe food, a good deal of which had become worse than bad from the\neffects of the hot climate, he had at once attributed to Salve this\npointed method of drawing his attention to the subject again"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 117,
      "char_start": 1798,
      "char_end": 2214,
      "text": "Elizabeth's face, and the still\nunexhausted might of early impressions, made him always shrink from the\nthought of having a murder on his conscience, and to that depth he never\nfell, deteriorated though his character gradually became, from daily\nassociation with everything that was vile, to that degree that he lost\nall power of believing in the existence of good amongst his\nfellow-creatures, or in a higher Power"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 127,
      "char_start": 399,
      "char_end": 809,
      "text": "She had been\nbought by auction for a small sum at Faerder; and in shape resembled an\nold wooden shoe, in which her skipper venturesomely trudged across to\nHolland through the spring and winter storms, calculating that he and\nhis crew could always lash themselves to something to avoid being washed\noverboard; that their timber cargo would keep them afloat; and that as\nlong as the rigging held they could sail"
    }
  ],
